// FIGHTER_PHOTOS: real-image overrides, keyed by characterKey, checked BEFORE the
// abstract SVG pictograms above. Embedded as base64 data URIs so the single-file
// HTML deliverable stays single-file (no external asset fetch, consistent with
// shell.html's existing "no build step" bundling). Thirteen fighters now have
// one, each a real character image already sitting in the project's own files
// (cammy.jpg / ryu.jpg / ken.jpg / zangief.jpg from earlier sessions;
// axl.png / cviper.png / vega.png added 2026-08-15, PNG per explicit user
// request rather than the earlier four's JPEG; akuma.png / sol.jpg the same
// day; Chun-li.png / Millia.jpg / giovanna.jpg / mbison2.jpg added 2026-08-17)
// — NOT sourced from a wiki/
// Capcom's actual game assets, which is the specific thing an earlier session
// declined (see HANDOFF.md's copyright-policy note). Since these are raster
// photos, not line-art pictograms, the `accent-you`/`accent-cpu`/`neutral` CSS
// color classes (which recolor the SVG poses via `currentColor`) don't do
// anything to them — that's expected, not a bug: a photo can't be recolored the
// way a stroke-based pictogram can. At the 20px board-token size a detailed
// photo reads as a tiny thumbnail rather than a clean silhouette — an accepted
// trade-off, not something to "fix" by shrinking/cropping further without being
// asked.
// The first four source files (cammy/ryu/ken/zangief) all had the exact same
// defect: a hard-edged, full-width, pure-white 8px strip along the very bottom
// edge only (confirmed via pixel inspection — a clean jump from near-black
// background straight to 255,255,255 with no gradient, unlike every other edge)
// — an export/padding artifact from whatever tool generated them, cropped off
// before embedding. The three newer files (axl/cviper/vega) were checked the
// same way and did NOT have it — so this is a property of whichever pipeline
// generated the first batch, not a universal property of every future photo.
// Still worth checking any newly swapped-in source file's edges before
// embedding it blind, just don't assume the defect is guaranteed anymore.
// The axl/cviper/vega/akuma/sol files were all resized down from their
// original resolution to 480px wide (the largest this project ever actually
// renders a fighter photo at — see `.fighter-card{width:230px}` in
// shell.html, the full-bleed poster background, doubled for high-DPI
// headroom) before embedding — shipping full original resolution in a
// single-file HTML deliverable wastes page weight for pixels nothing ever
// displays. Lossless resize only (kept full true color, no palette/indexed
// quantization — tried it once for axl/cviper/vega, GDI+'s default
// 8bpp-indexed conversion has no proper dithering and visibly posterizes
// photographic content; not worth the size savings for how bad it looked).
// akuma.png's source had an unused alpha channel (confirmed fully opaque by
// sampling, not assumed) — dropped to 24bpp RGB during the resize, same
// "don't ship bytes nothing needs" reasoning as the resolution cut. sol.jpg
// was already JPEG (kept that format rather than converting to PNG, since
// JPEG is legitimately the better fit for a photographic image and the file
// was provided that way) — resized the same way and re-encoded at quality 88.
// The 2026-08-17 batch (chunli/mbison/millia/giovanna) applied every lesson
// above up front rather than rediscovering them: all four checked for the
// bottom white strip first (none had it), all four embedded as JPEG at
// quality 88 rather than PNG — the axl/cviper/vega PNG choice was a one-off
// user request for that batch, and PNG costs ~5-10x the bytes for the same
// photographic content in a file that is already megabytes of base64. Three
// were resized to the usual 480px wide; mbison2.jpg arrived at 452px wide,
// already under the target, so it was embedded at native size and NOT
// re-encoded at all (upscaling would invent pixels and a JPEG->JPEG re-encode
// would only add generation loss for no size win).
